        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<br>
The maximum hydraulic and organic loading limits of attached growth, biological wastewater treatment systems have not been well defined, particularly for gravity independent systems designed for space applications. In addition, the useful lifetime of these biofilm systems for a given crew size, mission duration, and wastewater stream is unknown. The main goal of this project was to answer the question: What is the minimum size of the anoxic reactor required to treat a given crew’s wastewater for a specified period of time? To answer this question, organic loadings were varied by increasing hydraulic loading of a constant influent organic concentration representing urine, urine flush, humidity condensate, and hygiene water.  The limitations of the biological system were determined based on system performance, effluent quality, and measured organic and nitrogen removal efficiencies. The results of this study will provide design guidelines for sizing anoxic bioreactors to remove organic carbon and nitrogen from wastewaters containing personal cleansing agents, humidity condensate, and urine. <p>
Specific Aims:<br>
1.	Determine the maximum hydraulic loading rate to the pressurized predenitrification BWP system. <br>2. Determine the steady-state organic carbon and total nitrogen removed by the anoxic denitrification bioreactors operating at the maximum hydraulic loading rate. <br>3. Estimate the rate of biomass accumulation and the effects of this accumulation on accessible surface area, working volume, pressure drop, and treatment performance of the denitrification bioreactor. <br>4. Determine the lifetime of the anoxic denitrification bioreactor operating at the maximum hydraulic loading rate. <br>5. Estimate the minimum size of the anoxic denitrification bioreactor to treat EPB wastewater for a given crew size and mission duration. 
<p>
APPROACH:<br>
